WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.570
"Benefits of Quinoa for Lowering Triglycerides"

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.179
Approximately 90% of the
world's calories are provided

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:17.410
by less than 1% of the quarter
million known edible plants.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.869
The big three are wheat, corn,
and rice, the reliance upon which

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:26.020
may be unsustainable given
the ongoing climate crisis.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.610
This has spurned new interest into
underutilized crops like quinoa,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.430
which might do better
with the drought and heat.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:37.360
Quinoa has only been introduced in
the Northern hemisphere recently,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.780
but humans have been eating
quinoa for more than 7,000 years.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.020
Is there any truth to the
superfood designation

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:50.600
or is it all just marketing hooey?
Quinoa is a pseudo-grain,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.350
since the plant it comes
from isn't a type of grass.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.800
Technically it's a seed-like fruit.
It does have a lot of protein,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.830
and also lots of vitamins and minerals,
but so do all whole grains.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.509
Yeah, it has more protein
than other grains,

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:08.700
but since when do we
need more protein?

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.380
Fiber is what we're sorely lacking,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.299
and its fiber content is relatively
modest compared to barley or rye.

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:18.240
Pretty strong on folate and vitamin E,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.250
and leads the pack on
magnesium, iron and zinc.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.200
So nutritious?
Sure, but when I think superfood,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.720
I think some sort of
special clinical benefit.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.440
So broccoli is a superfood, strawberries
are a superfood, garlic is a superfood.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.520
But what about quinoa?

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.530
Consumer demand is up, thanks in
part to perceived health benefits.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.219
In lab animals it has all
sorts of purported benefits,

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:45.529
but there have been
very few human studies.

00:01:45.529 --> 00:01:51.229
The first trial was a before and after
study of quinoa granola bars

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:56.340
that showed drops in triglycerides and
cholesterol, but with no control group

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.710
you don't know how much of that would
have happened without the quinoa.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:05.709
This is the kind of study I wanted to
see: a randomized controlled trial.

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:08.840
And about a cup a day of
cooked quinoa for 12 weeks

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.600
led to a 36% drop in triglycerides.
That's comparable to what one gets

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:19.170
with triglyceride-lowering drugs
or high dose fish oil supplements.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.879
Which is better, regular
quinoa or red quinoa?

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:27.470
Well, red does have about
twice the antioxidant power,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.400
leading the investigators to conclude
that red quinoa might significantly

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.340
contribute to the management and/or
prevention of degenerative diseases

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.269
associated with free radical damage,
though it's never been put to the test.

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:42.349
What about black quinoa?

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:45.879
Both red and black appear
equally antioxidant rich,

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:48.450
both beating out the more
conventional white.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.510
The only caveat I could find
is to inform your doctor

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:58.110
before your next colonoscopy, else
they might mistake it for parasites.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:03.290
A colonoscopy revealed numerous
egg-like tan-yellow ovoid objects

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:08.800
of unclear cause, but it
was just undigested quinoa.

